// =============================================================================
// ZESTRY. STAGE 1 (v2): Accounts, businesses, onboarding, memberships
// EXPAND phase: additive or loosening changes only.
// =============================================================================
// What this supports:
//   * Registration: a personal account (first name, last name, other names,
//     phone required, email optional).
//   * Business Hub: list every business the signed-in user belongs to, with
//     their role, and resume any business that is still being set up.
//   * Business onboarding: business created when onboarding starts, progress
//     saved, a location (Site) always created, vertical chosen as business type.
//   * Offline later: stable internal IDs, memberships and role assignments with
//     business/site scope, and an identity link that does not depend on Clerk.
//
// Release plan (expand and contract):
//   1. EXPAND: apply this schema + the backfill SQL. The currently running app
//      keeps working because nothing it uses is dropped or renamed.
//   2. NEW APP: deploy the new app. It reads identity from the new tables and,
//      for rollback safety only, also keeps the legacy User columns populated
//      where they can represent the data (one business per user, role in
//      OWNER / MANAGER / CASHIER). Re-run the backfill right before cutover.
//   3. CONTRACT: after a soak period, stop writing the legacy columns and apply
//      the contract migration (shown at the bottom).
//
// Rollback caveat: the legacy columns can describe only ONE business per user
// and only three roles. Once any user belongs to two businesses, or a
// restaurant role (chef, waiter) exists, treat the new app as a one-way door.
//
// How to apply: replace your existing `Business` and `User` models with the
// versions below and add everything else. Everything marked NEW is new,
// everything marked CHANGED is additive or loosening, and LEGACY marks columns
// that the contract release removes.
// =============================================================================

// --- NEW enums ---------------------------------------------------------------

// Each business has exactly one vertical (never both). Chosen in onboarding
// as the "business type". Add a value (for example HOTEL) only when a vertical
// is actually supported.

// --- CHANGED: Business -------------------------------------------------------

// --- CHANGED: User (the person's Zestry account) ----------------------------
// The account represents the individual, not a business. One account can own
// one business and manage another.

// --- NEW: Site ---------------------------------------------------------------
// A location (outlet or branch) of a business. Every business gets at least
// one, created during onboarding step 2 (for example "Main Branch"), even if it
// has a single outlet. Devices will bind to exactly one site later. The label
// shown to users ("Location", "Branch", "Store") is a per-vertical choice.

// --- NEW: ExternalIdentity ---------------------------------------------------
// Link from a user to an identity-provider account. Keeps the app independent
// of Clerk: the rest of the system uses User.id. Staff who never sign in online
// (offline PIN users, later) have no row here.

// --- NEW: Membership ---------------------------------------------------------
// A person's relationship to ONE business. This is what the Business Hub lists.
// Disabling a membership ends the person's authority everywhere in that
// business. Business and its first (owner) membership must be created in one
// database transaction so a business never exists without an owner.

// --- NEW: RoleAssignment -----------------------------------------------------
// A role at business scope (siteId null) or at one site. roleKey is validated
// against role definitions kept in code, not a database enum, so adding a role
// (for example chef or waiter) needs no migration. businessId is repeated so
// every tenant-owned row carries it.
//
// Known limitation: Prisma cannot express "unique when siteId is null", so the
// app must prevent duplicate business-wide assignments.

// =============================================================================
// CONTRACT RELEASE: do NOT apply until the new app has been live and verified,
// and the app no longer writes the legacy columns.
// =============================================================================
// User becomes:
//
// model User {
//   id                 String             @id @default(cuid())
//   firstName          String
//   lastName           String?
//   otherNames         String?
//   phone              String?            @unique
//   email              String?            @unique
//   createdAt          DateTime           @default(now())
//   updatedAt          DateTime           @updatedAt
//
//   salesAsCashier     Sale[]             @relation("SaleCashier")
//   stockMovements     StockMovement[]    @relation("StockMovementCreatedBy")
//   expensesRecorded   Expense[]          @relation("ExpenseCreatedBy")
//   externalIdentities ExternalIdentity[]
//   memberships        Membership[]
//
//   @@map("users")
// }
//
// Business: remove `users User[]`, remove `businessType` (after reviewing it),
// and remove the defaults on `vertical` and `status`.
//
// Generate it with `prisma migrate dev --create-only`, review the SQL, take a
// backup, then apply. See Section C of stage1_backfill_and_verify.sql.
// =============================================================================
